II, scene ii]:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) Associate; fellow; of the same condition.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": ["His even servant."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["flat", "level", "uniform", "regular", "monotone", "level", "on par", "round", "quits", "quits", "square"], "antonyms": ["uneven", "odd"]}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈiː.vən/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈi.vən/", "tags": ["US"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈi.vn̩]", "tags": ["US"]}], "forms": [{"word": "evens", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "evening", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "evened", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "evened", "tags": ["past"]}, {"word": "eben", "tags": ["alternative"]}, {"word": "e'en", "tags": ["alternative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(transitive) To make flat and level.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["We need to even this playing field; the west goal is too low."], "quotes": [{"text": "This temple Xerxes evened with the soil.", "reference": "1614, Walter Ralegh [i.e., Walter Raleigh], The Historie of the World […], London: […] William Stansby for Walter Burre, […], →OCLC, (please specify |book=1 to 5):"}, {"text": "[...] It will now be good to Beat, Roll, and Mow Carpet-walks, and Cammomile; for now the ground is ſupple, and it will even all inequalities: [...]", "reference": "1669, John Evelyn, “Kalendarium Hortense: OrThe Gard’ners Almanac; […] [October.]”, in Sylva, or A Discourse of Forest-trees and the Propagation of Timber in His Majesties Dominions. […], 3rd edition, London: […] Jo[hn] Martyn, and Ja[mes] Allestry, printers to the Royal Society, →OCLC, page 27:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, obsolete) To equal or equate; to make the same.", "tags": ["obsolete", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "The Engliſh Earl [William Longespée the Younger], though he ſtood on the lower ground in point of birth, yet conceived himſelf to even him [Robert I, Count of Artois] in valour and martiall knowledge.", "reference": "1639, Thomas Fuller, “Discords betwixt the French and English; the Death & Disposition of Meladine King of Egypt”, in The Historie of the Holy Warre, Cambridge, Cambridgeshire: […] Thomas Buck, one of the printers to the Universitie of Cambridge [and sold by John Williams, London], →OCLC, book IV, page 192:"}, {"text": "“But aside from that,” I continued, “what have I done that you should even me to dogs by such a supposition? I never yet failed a friend, and it’s not likely I’ll begin with you. There are things between us that I can never forget, even if you can.”", "reference": "1886 May 1 – July 31, Robert Louis Stevenson, Kidnapped, being Memoirs of the Adventures of David Balfour in the Year 1751: […], London; Paris: Cassell & Company, published 1886, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive, obsolete) To be equal.", "tags": ["intransitive", "obsolete"], "examples": ["Thrice nine evens twenty seven."], "quotes": [{"text": "A redoubled numbering never eveneth with the first.", "reference": "1609, Richard Carew, The Survey of Cornwall, London: […] S[imon] S[tafford] for Iohn Iaggard, […], →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, obsolete) To place in an equal state, as to obligation, or in a state in which nothing is due on either side; to balance, as accounts; to make quits.", "tags": ["obsolete", "transitive"], "examples": ["We need to even the score."], "quotes": [{"text": "Madam, the care I have had to even your\ncontent I wish might be found in the calendar of my\npast endeavours, for then we wound our modesty, and\nmake foul the clearness of our deservings, when of\nourselves we publish them.", "reference": "c. 1604–05, William Shakespeare, All's Well that Ends Well, act 1, scene 3:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, obsolete) To set right; to complete.", "tags": ["obsolete", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, obsolete) To act up to; to keep pace with.", "tags": ["obsolete", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Prithee away,\nThere's more to be considered: but we'll even\nAll that good time will give us.", "reference": "c. 1611, William Shakespeare, Cymbeline, act 3, scene 4:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["flatten", "level", "match", "settle"], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "adverb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈiː.vən/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈi.vən/", "tags": ["US"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈi.vn̩]", "tags": ["US"]}], "forms": [{"word": "eben", "tags": ["alternative"]}, {"word": "e'en", "tags": ["alternative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(archaic) Exactly; just; fully.", "tags": ["archaic", "not comparable"], "examples": ["I fulfilled my instructions even as I had promised.", "You are leaving tonight? — Even so.", "This is my commandment, that ye love one another, even as I have loved you."], "quotes": [{"text": "But on the occasion in question, those dents looked deeper, even as his nervous step that morning left a deeper mark.", "reference": "1851 November 14, Herman Melville, chapter 36, in Moby-Dick; or, The Whale, 1st American edition, New York, N.Y.: Harper & Brothers; London: Richard Bentley, →OCLC, page 177:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "In reality (used to imply an extreme example in the case mentioned).", "tags": ["not comparable"], "examples": ["Even a blind squirrel finds an acorn sometimes.", "Did you even make it through the front door?", "That was before I was even born."], "quotes": [{"text": "He used to drop into my chambers once in a while to smoke, and was first-rate company. When I gave a dinner there was generally a cover laid for him. I liked the man for his own sake, and even had he promised to turn out a celebrity it would have had no weight with me.", "reference": "1897 December (indicated as 1898), Winston Churchill, chapter I, in The Celebrity: An Episode, New York, N.Y.: The Macmillan Company; London: Macmillan & Co., Ltd., →OCLC, page 2:"}, {"text": "Carried somehow, somewhither, for some reason, on these surging floods, were these travelers,[…]. Even such a boat as the Mount Vernon offered a total deck space so cramped as to leave secrecy or privacy well out of the question, even had the motley and democratic assemblage of passengers been disposed to accord either.", "reference": "1910, Emerson Hough, chapter II, in The Purchase Price: Or The Cause of Compromise, Indianapolis, Ind.: The Bobbs-Merrill Company, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "He read the letter aloud. Sophia listened with the studied air of one for whom, even in these days, a title possessed some surreptitious allurement.", "reference": "1921, Ben Travers, chapter 1, in A Cuckoo in the Nest, Garden City, N.Y.: Doubleday, Page & Company, published 1925, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "Since the mid-1980s, when Indonesia first began to clear its bountiful forests on an industrial scale in favour of lucrative palm-oil plantations, “haze” has become an almost annual occurrence in South-East Asia. The cheapest way to clear logged woodland is to burn it, producing an acrid cloud of foul white smoke that, carried by the wind, can cover hundreds, or even thousands, of square miles.", "reference": "2013 June 29, “Unspontaneous combustion”, in The Economist, volume 407, number 8842, page 29:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "Used to emphasise a comparative.", "tags": ["not comparable"], "examples": ["I was strong before, but now I am even stronger."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "Used to indicate a further degree of comparison.", "tags": ["not comparable"], "examples": ["Bob is taller than Alice, but Cameron is even taller than Bob."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "Rather; that is (used to signal a correction of a previous utterance).", "tags": ["not comparable"], "examples": ["My favorite actor is Jack Nicklaus. Jack Nicholson, even."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["definitely", "precisely", "so much as", "still", "in other words"], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈiː.vən/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈi.vən/", "tags": ["US"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈi.vn̩]", "tags": ["US"]}], "forms": [{"word": "evens", "tags": ["plural"]}, {"word": "eben", "tags": ["alternative"]}, {"word": "e'en", "tags": ["alternative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(mathematics, diminutive) An even number.", "tags": ["diminutive"], "examples": ["So let's see. There are two evens here and three odds."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈiː.vən/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈi.vən/", "tags": ["US"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈi.vn̩]", "tags": ["US"]}], "forms": [{"word": "evens", "tags": ["plural"]}, {"word": "eben", "tags": ["alternative"]}, {"word": "e'en", "tags": ["alternative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(archaic or poetic) Evening.", "tags": ["archaic", "poetic"], "examples": ["We'll meet at even, when the sun is set."], "quotes": [{"text": "Whẽ the evẽ was come they bꝛought vnto hĩ many that were poſſeſſed with devyllꝭ / […]", "reference": "1526, [William Tyndale, transl.], The Newe Testamẽt […] (Tyndale Bible), [Worms, Germany: Peter Schöffer], →OCLC, Matthew viij:[16], folio x, recto:"}, {"text": "When ſparkling ſtars twire not thou guil'ſt th' eauen.", "reference": "1609, William Shakespeare, “Sonnet 28”, in Shake-speares Sonnets. […], London: By G[eorge] Eld for T[homas] T[horpe] and are to be sold by William Aspley, →OCLC, signature C2, verso:"}, {"text": "And if any mans ſeede of copulation goe out from him, then hee ſhall waſh all his fleſh in water, and bee vncleane vntill the Euen.", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, Leviticus 15:16, signature L2, verso, column 2:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "af", "name": "Afrikaans"}, "word": "aand"}, {"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "вечер"}, {"language": {"code": "cs", "name": "Czech"}, "word": "večer"}, {"language": {"code": "nl", "name": "Dutch"}, "word": "avondstond"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "ilta"}, {"language": {"code": "fr", "name": "French"}, "word": "veillée"}, {"language": {"code": "fr", "name": "French"}, "word": "veillée du soir"}, {"language": {"code": "he", "name": "Hebrew"}, "word": "ערב"}, {"language": {"code": "it", "name": "Italian"}, "word": "sera"}, {"language": {"code": "la", "name": "Latin"}, "word": "vesper"}, {"language": {"code": "pl", "name": "Polish"}, "word": "poranek"}, {"language": {"code": "ru", "name": "Russian"}, "word": "ве́чер"}, {"language": {"code": "es", "name": "Spanish"}, "word": "tarde"}, {"language": {"code": "sv", "name": "Swedish"}, "word": "kväll"}, {"language": {"code": "sv", "name": "Swedish"}, "word": "afton"}, {"language": {"code": "vi", "name": "Vietnamese"}, "word": "chiều"}], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["evening", "eventide"], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/even", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}